ID: B2-Ch10b-Blame
TEXT:

#if(v_hearing >= 2) {
 

As we near the destination, the woods go unnatural. By every measure of my boosted hearing, a camp this size ought to hum with voices, footsteps, clattering gear—yet not a leaf rustles ahead. A silencing ward blankets the perimeter, sealed tight as a drum, thick and airless the way Melindra's tent felt two nights ago.

Through the tree line, four sheer slabs of solid rock loom twenty feet high, boxing the clearing into a fortress. An earth elementalist raised them straight from the bedrock, seamless and raw.

"There's the entrance, over there," Nolderan says, pointing toward a heavy stone door set into the wall.

"Quite the impressive camp fortifications you have here, Nolderan," Hadrik says.

"Yes, we have a very talented earth elementalist in our team," Nolderan says. "Step right in."

He heaves the stone door open. We file inside one by one into an open compound, and the five mages of his squad come into full view. Two hundred feet across the dirt, a dwarf sits behind a slab table on a stone chair, tipping back a bottle of dwarven ale. Four bodyguards stand shoulder to shoulder behind him.

}
#if(v_hearing < 2) {
 

Through the tree line, four sheer slabs of solid rock loom twenty feet high, boxing the clearing into a fortress. An earth elementalist raised them straight from the bedrock, seamless and raw.

"There's the entrance, over there," Nolderan says, pointing toward a heavy stone door set into the wall.

"Quite the impressive camp fortifications you have here, Nolderan," Hadrik says.

"Yes, we have a very talented earth elementalist in our team," Nolderan says. "Step right in."

He heaves the stone door open. We file inside one by one into an open compound, and the five mages of his squad come into full view. Two hundred feet across the dirt, a dwarf sits behind a slab table on a stone chair, tipping back a bottle of dwarven ale. Four bodyguards stand shoulder to shoulder behind him.

}
 The dwarf lowers his bottle, and his features click into place. Every newspaper across the continents has run that broad, bearded face for twelve years. Sitting right in front of us is Talmak, the Sage of the North.

Arraka bursts into shrill laughter from the amulet.

"Talmak?..." Daren's voice drops into disbelief.

Flower's eyes glow bright blue, and Illuna looks between Daren and the dwarf with open contempt. "The Sage of the North? THIS is the almighty sage you've been talking about?..."

Arraka cackles louder, rattling against the metal casing.

Illuna catches the chain in her fist and glares down at the amulet. "You knew about this, didn't you? You knew one of the mages was a sage from the start!"

"Of course I knew!" Arraka wheezes through shrieks of delight. "But I wanted to see the looks on your faces when you saw him for yourselves! Ahahahahaha!"

"Master Nolderan..." Daren's jaw tightens. "Why didn't you tell us the sage was on your team?"

"If I had told you this, would you have followed me here, boy?" Nolderan says.

"You should not blame him for this, healer in armor," Talmak says, his voice carrying easily across the stone yard as he thumps his bottle onto the table. "Your old master works for me now. He does what I tell him. But do not worry, I did not call you here to fight. I called you here because I wanted to talk. Now, come closer. All of you. I don't like having to shout to make myself heard."

We trade glances and start across the empty dirt toward the stone table, Nolderan pacing beside us. Two paces in, Melindra snatches Flower by the wrist and grabs my sleeve, yanking us back. The smirk is gone from her face; her eyes are dead level.

"After I snap my fingers, I'm going to need you two to pull me out," Melindra whispers. "Do you understand?"

choice(""I guess so..."", B2-Ch10b-Guess, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Guess)
choice(""Pull you out of what?..."", B2-Ch10b-Pull, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Pull)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Guess
TEXT:

 "I guess so..."

"Good," Melindra murmurs. She brushes past, and the familiar faint pressure of her air currents settles around my limbs, identical to the morning's spell.

She strides ahead to rejoin the squad without a backward glance. Flower and I exchange a brief, baffled look before jogging after her toward the stone table.

The air current sits dormant, tracking my stride, waiting for my arms to lift before kicking in. Closer to the table, Talmak's squad comes into sharp focus. The sage stands roughly Hadrik's height in dwarf form, broad-shouldered, with a thick brown beard hiding most of his jaw. Aside from the title, nothing about his posture or frame suggests an overwhelming threat; Illuna's disappointment makes sense.

He wears heavily enchanted leather armor identical to Nolderan's, and the four behind him share the same kit, all empty-handed. Nolderan's enchanting craft runs through every stitch.

Behind the ex-sage stand a banshee, an elf, and two dwarves. The two dwarves look far nastier than their master: one bears twin diagonal scars across his cheek, the other an eyepatch over a sunken socket. The elf is tall, lean, with pale blond hair and a network of deep wrinkles around his eyes—a rare sight on an elf, marking him as well past a century old.

The banshee's host is a woman in her early thirties, dark-haired, with emerald light burning steadily behind her pupils the way Illuna's blue glow fills Flower's gaze. An eerie, unearthly aura radiates off her; this is no artificial creation from the Beacon.

The awakening is long finished, the original host soul gone, leaving the banshee in sole control. Yet she holds human shape without rotting into the twisted forms usually born from a full takeover. Not just Flower and Illuna, then. My understanding of banshees still has gaps.

Her black hair hangs loose, her face hard and unyielding. As we halt, her green gaze flickers to Flower for half a second before snapping forward, rigid, waiting on Talmak.

We stop twenty paces short of the table, spreading out to leave room for footwork and wards if steel comes out. Nolderan walks past us, taking his place in the bodyguard line behind the sage.

"I see that you are not willing to advance any further," Talmak says, taking another pull from his ale. "No matter. At least you are close enough that I don't need to shout. Let's get down to business, then, shall we?"

He sets the bottle down and plants both palms on the stone.

"I think you may have already guessed that all of the pinecone-collecting mages outside are working for me," Talmak says. "They are all mages who have agreed to give up on participating in this tournament, and to spend the rest of their time here helping me win instead. In exchange for their services, they will be receiving generous rewards, many of them unobtainable by normal means. As you all know, when you become a sage, you receive many benefits. You also gain a lot of influence, with many people. And I can use this influence, to make people's wishes come true. Now tell me, Daren, would you not by any chance have any wishes that you would want to come true?"

"My wish is to gain access to the Magium," Daren says, each word cold and deliberate. "But from what I'm gathering, this is the one wish that you would not want to grant me..."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Recruited, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Recruited)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Pull
TEXT:

 "Pull you out of what?..."

"You'll understand when the time comes," Melindra murmurs. She brushes past, and the familiar faint pressure of her air currents settles around my limbs, identical to the morning's spell.

She strides ahead to rejoin the squad without a backward glance. Flower and I exchange a brief, baffled look before jogging after her toward the stone table.

The air current sits dormant, tracking my stride, waiting for my arms to lift before kicking in. Closer to the table, Talmak's squad comes into sharp focus. The sage stands roughly Hadrik's height in dwarf form, broad-shouldered, with a thick brown beard hiding most of his jaw. Aside from the title, nothing about his posture or frame suggests an overwhelming threat; Illuna's disappointment makes sense.

