choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Recruited, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Recruited)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Recruited
TEXT:

 "Don't be a fool, boy!" Nolderan snaps. "This is a once in a lifetime opportunity. You will never get a chance like this again. For once in your life, use that rusted brain of yours and think!"

"I think I understand what's going on here," Daren says, his eyes fixing on Nolderan. "It wasn't an accident that you found our camp, was it, master Nolderan? You knew where I'd be. The sage sent you to bring me in so he could make his pitch. I thought I made myself clear when I left your letter unanswered, Sage Talmak. Why send for me again?"

"He's made this offer to you before?" Hadrik asks, eyebrows raised.

"Yes," Daren says. "A letter arrived right after the tournament was proclaimed. Master Nolderan must have received one too. Except he accepted."

"Why aren't you being honest with me, Daren?" Talmak says, swirling the dregs of his bottle. "Why don't you tell me the real reason why you won't join me? Who else managed to recruit you before I did? Was it Azarius? Nolderan's told me that you've always admired him. But I can offer you more than Azarius ever could. You have no idea how much knowledge you gain access to when you are a sage. There are so many books that are not shown to the general public. So many restricted areas. I could give you access to all of these. And all I'm asking in return is for you to join my team, and to help me win the Magium. Hell, I'll even extend the offer to all your friends, if you want. You only need to say yes."

"I wasn't recruited by Azarius!" Daren's voice rises, raw and furious. "Don't you understand? I came here on my own! I came here because I'm tired of travelling around the world, trying to save people, while thousands of others are dying somewhere else, where I cannot reach them. No matter how much I trained, or how hard I worked, it was never enough. I couldn't save them all. But once I get the Magium, I may finally become able to achieve my dreams..."

"Boy, are you out of your mind?" Nolderan shouts. "Are you telling me that you are still holding on to your foolish ideals from back when you were still my pupil? When will you finally grow up and accept reality?"

"Grow up?..." Daren takes a step forward, plate armor grinding. "And then what? Become Talmak's lapdog, like you did? What happened to you? What happened to the master I knew, who was a proud man that was happy and content with teaching people martial arts and enchanting for a living? What happened to that man who would never bend his knee to anyone, regardless of their rank or their strength?"

"That man died long ago," Nolderan says, his voice dropping flat and bitter. "He died after I lost my sage title, and after everyone abandoned me, forcing me to take up work as a mercenary. But this time it's different. I'm no longer just working for scraps. If we succeed, here, I could finally turn my life around. And so could you, if you wouldn't keep stubbornly holding on to those childish ideals that you've had ever since I first met you!"

"It seems that my old master truly is dead..." Daren says, shaking his head. "But my ideals are not. I am sorry to say that I will be refusing your offer once more, Sage of the North."

"Surely, you must be joking..." Talmak says, staring at Daren in sheer disbelief. "You can't seriously be thinking that you have any chance of winning this tournament by yourself, when there are four sages competing against you... There's no way that anyone can be this naive."

"He is serious," Nolderan says quietly. "I can see it in his eyes. It's the same look that he had when he first told me why he wanted to become my disciple. It hasn't changed one bit since then..."

"No, this can't be possible..." Talmak rubs his temples. "I refuse to believe that this is actually happening... Someone needs to wake this man up! You there! The one without any magical powers. I don't know if you're Daren's servant, or his advisor, but you seem like a man with his head on his shoulders. Try to make him see reason. Please!"

choice(""Yeah, Daren, you should quit the tournament. Less competition for me, that way."", B2-Ch10b-Compete, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Compete, v_b2_ch10_advisor = 0)
choice(""Daren's servant? Do you have any idea how many times I saved this guy's neck?"", B2-Ch10b-Compete, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Compete, v_b2_ch10_advisor = 1)
choice(""You asked the wrong man to talk him down. I'm here for the Magium. So is he."", B2-Ch10b-Compete, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Compete, v_b2_ch10_advisor = 2)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Compete
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch10_advisor == 0) {
 "Yeah, Daren, you should quit the tournament. Less competition for me, that way."

"You won't get rid of me that easily, you opportunistic bastard," Daren says, a sharp grin flashing across his face.

Talmak blinks, his brow furrowing. "Is this supposed to be some sort of joke? Please tell me that you are not seriously planning to participate in this tournament against me as well..."

"It's not a joke..." Kate's voice is dead flat. "Take a good look at them, and you will see from their eyes that they are not bluffing. They really did both come here to win the tournament."

Talmak's head jerks between me and Daren, reading the lack of hesitation on either of our faces. The realization hits him like a slap, and his composure breaks into a furious snarl.

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_advisor == 1) {
 "Daren's servant? Do you have any idea how many times I saved this guy's neck?"

"Not as many times as I saved yours," Daren shoots back.

"Really, now?" I grin. "Well, maybe we should start keeping count from now on! What do you say?"

"Sounds good to me," Daren says.

"What are you two blabbering about?" Talmak barks. "If this person isn't Daren's servant or advisor, then what did he come here for?"

"He came here to win the Magium," Daren says. "Just like I did."

Talmak turns his full gaze on me, measuring my lack of armor and ordinary frame. "Is this supposed to be some sort of joke? Please tell me that you are not seriously planning to participate in this tournament against me as well..."

"It's not a joke..." Kate's voice is dead flat. "Take a good look at them, and you will see from their eyes that they are not bluffing. They really did both come here to win the tournament."

Talmak's head jerks between me and Daren, reading the lack of hesitation on either of our faces. The realization hits him like a slap, and his composure breaks into a furious snarl.

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_advisor == 2) {
 "You asked the wrong man to talk him down. I'm here for the Magium. So is he."

"You? Competing for the Magium?..." Talmak sneers, looking me up and down with utter contempt. "You have no magical power whatsoever. Daren, what kind of delusion have you dragged along with you?"

"Hey, you can ignore me all you want, but that won't make the truth go away," I say.

"Actually, Barry didn't come here with me," Daren says. "He joined the tournament by himself, and he is also aiming to win the Magium. He is a participant, just like the rest of us."

"Please tell me that you are joking..." Talmak scowls. "This has to be a joke!"

"It's not a joke..." Kate's voice is dead flat. "Take a good look at them, and you will see from their eyes that they are not bluffing. They really did both come here to win the tournament."

Talmak's head jerks between me and Daren, reading the lack of hesitation on either of our faces. The realization hits him like a slap, and his composure breaks into a furious snarl.

}
 "You would compete against me?" Talmak roars, surging to his feet. "ME? The Sage of the North?"

The suppression drops from his aura. The raw magical pressure slams outward like a shockwave, heavy as stone, pressing straight against my chest.

"As far as intimidating auras go, I've seen better," I say, steadying my breath.

"Do you people think this is a game?" Talmak's fists clench on the stone table, his knuckles white. "Do you all think that this offer I made is something that you can simply refuse? Are you unable to understand the situation you are in?"

"I think we all understand our situation very well, Sage Talmak," Daren says, his hand dropping to his hilt. "Judging by the terrified looks that those mages outside were giving us when they saw us travelling alongside Nolderan, I'm willing to bet that you've also threatened them in the same way, and that this is the real reason why you have so many people working for you. But these sorts of tactics won't work on me."

"Is that so?" Talmak's tone drops dangerously low. "Well, Nolderan, I hope you won't hate me too much if I kill your old disciple. He is simply leaving me no choice, at this point."

"The boy chose this path on his own," Nolderan says, not looking at Daren. "He was given ample opportunities to see reason, but he wouldn't listen. I am sad that it has come to this, but there is no other way."

"It's good to see that you still care, master Nolderan," Daren says, the irony biting deep.

"These two fools have already sealed their fate!" Talmak turns his glare on the rest of the group. "But the rest of you do not need to die together with them. If you join my side now, you will be allowed to live."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Denied, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Denied)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Denied
TEXT:

 "That's mighty generous of you," Hadrik says, grinning broadly. "But I'm afraid that I'm going to have to refuse."

"What about you, banshees?" Talmak looks between Kate and Flower. "Are you going to stand by your friend's side and get slaughtered, or will you pick the sane choice, and live?"

"I did not come here to become a mercenary," Kate says, her stance shifting into balance. "You are not the first powerful adversary that has threatened us since we've arrived in Varathia, and you will not be the last."

"One banshee has denied me, but one remains..." Talmak says, his eyes narrowing at Flower. "What do you say, little girl? Will you also make the same reckless choice as your friends? Or will you choose the winning side?"

"I think that you might be greatly overestimating yourself, Sage of the North," Illuna says, cold and dismissive.

Talmak gives a harsh, dry laugh. "So you will all die together, then..."

"But what about me?" Melindra steps forward, clasping her hands with a wide, trembling gaze. "Are you going to kill me too? I am only a defenseless maiden who was being escorted by these warriors. I do not want to die..."

"Hmm?..." Talmak blinks down at her, as if registering her presence for the first time. "Oh, I suppose there's no need for you to die with them if you don't want to."

"Then... can I come to your side of the table?" Melindra asks, small and hesitant.

"Of course," Talmak says. "And the other girl can come to this side as well, if she wants."

"I will stay here," Leila writes in glowing blue script as Melindra walks across the open dirt.

Talmak frowns at the floating letters for a second, then nods. "As you wish."

"I should have known that you would abandon us with the first opportunity you got, you traitor!" Kate spits at Melindra's back.

"Don't take it personally, ice mage," Melindra says with a sweet smirk, slipping into line beside Talmak. "I did somewhat enjoy travelling with you and your group. But now it's time to move on. I'm sure that I will find other suitable candidates to escort me to Ollendor, eventually."

Arraka's howling laughter erupts from the amulet again.

"What is it that you are finding so amusing?" Illuna snaps down at the chain.

"Don't you see?" Arraka cackles. "Don't you see what is going to happen?"

Talmak ignores the amulet entirely, raising his voice to carry across the yard. "Well, it would appear that everyone has now chosen their side. Is there anything else you might want to say, before we commence this massacre?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Vague, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Vague)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Vague
TEXT:

 The pieces don't fit. Melindra doesn't pull people aside for vague rescue warnings only to switch sides thirty seconds later. And if she truly wanted safety, she'd take to the sky and clear out entirely. The only reason to cross the line is proximity.

"Just shut up and prepare yourself for battle, will you?" Daren unhooks his shield, drawing his enchanted broadsword in one smooth motion.

Arraka's shrieking laughter rings out louder behind us.

"Then let us begin..." Talmak sneers.

"Yes," Melindra's smirk sharpens into steel. "Let us begin!"

Both her hands come up, snapping her fingers in unison.

A violent roar of wind detonates where she stands. A monstrous tornado erupts instantly around her, tearing the stone table apart and swallowing Talmak, Nolderan, and all four bodyguards into its roaring funnel before anyone can chant a ward. The vortex spins them skyward in a dizzying spiral, its base widening with every second as it gouges deep ruts into the earth.

"I knew it!" Arraka howls from the amulet. "I knew that she'd do it the moment she asked to join the sage's side. This girl is even crazier than the half-lessathi. I'm dying of laughter, here, ahahahaha!"

"But... why?" Kate stares up at the howling column in disbelief. "Why did she do it?"

"Everyone get back!" I yell over the gale. "Melindra can't control her powers properly. This tornado may well engulf us too, soon, if we don't get away from it."

"Can't control her powers properly?..." Kate retreats a pace, eyes wide. "Wait, is this why she's never fought together with us in the past? Because she could risk hurting us by accident?"

"Yes," I say. "Now let's move!"

"But if you knew about this... then why didn't you tell us?" Kate demands as we sprint back toward the perimeter wall.

"Because she asked me to keep this a secret," I shout back. "Listen, I don't have time to explain now. We'll discuss this in more detail later. Flower, we need to fly up there and get Melindra out of the tornado."

"What?..." Flower blinks.

"She asked us to pull her out after she snaps her fingers, remember?" I say. "Now come on. We need to hurry!"

I throw both arms upward. The dormant air currents catch immediately, lifting me off the dirt with a surging draft. Flower ignites her fire jets with a low hiss, rocketing up beside me as we angle toward the swirling wall of dust. High on the outer edge of the vortex, Melindra's silhouette spins past in rapid circles.

choice("Go and pull her out of there immediately.", B2-Ch10b-Immediately, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Immediately)
choice("Confirm that all the other mages are still inside the tornado, before pulling her out.", B2-Ch10b-Confirm, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Confirm)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Immediately
TEXT:

 "Come on, let's get her out!" Flower says.

We drive upward through the turbulence, pulling level with the outer rim of the vortex. As the rotation whips Melindra toward our side, I shout over the howling wind.

"Melindra! Reach out on the next turn!"

Melindra holds her breath, eyes watering against the dust, and gives a sharp nod. As the cyclone carries her around in a tight arc, she thrusts both arms outward. Flower and I each seize a wrist and heave with all our weight, tearing her free of the vortex into open air. We fly twenty yards clear before easing down to let her recover.

"Took you long enough..." Melindra coughs violently, spitting grit from her mouth.

"We came as fast as we could," I say.

"No doubt," she wheezes, rubbing her eyes. "Still felt like ten years in a rock grinder. Glad to see you got the message, at least."

"Understood eventually," I say. "A little more detail wouldn't have hurt."

"I couldn't risk it," Melindra says. "Aura-readers in that squad would have caught a lie in half a heartbeat. I needed genuine panic on your faces for the bluff to hold."

"Could you not cast the storm from our side?" I ask. "Why walk straight into their line?"

"A funnel that size needs an anchor," Melindra says. "If I'm not dead center or right above the eye, the current tears itself apart."

"And now?" I ask. "Can you hold it from here?"

"Should be able to," Melindra mutters. "The initial burst is the hard part. Maintaining it is just—ugh..."

Her hand snaps to her temple, her face twisting in pain.

"Are you alright?" Flower asks.

"Fine, just..." Melindra rubs her forehead with trembling fingers. "Pushed too much mana into the cast. Give me a second and the ache will—oh no."

Below us, three miniature dust devils sprout from the dirt, spinning erratically within spitting distance of Daren and Kate.

"No, no, no!" Melindra's voice spikes. "The stray currents are slipping! I have to drop them, now!"

"Just don't drop the main—"

Before the sentence leaves my mouth, the central tornado collapses into a dead gust. The mini-twisters vanish instantly with it.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Fascinated, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Fascinated)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Confirm
TEXT:

 "Come on, let's get her out!" Flower says.

"Hold on," I say, scanning the dust. "Make sure the other six are still trapped inside. If someone slipped free, they'll drop on us the second we close in."

"Hurry, then!" Flower urges. "She can't breathe in that dirt forever!"

We circle the perimeter for twenty seconds, tracking the dark silhouettes tumbling through the roaring funnel until all six enemies are accounted for. Cleared of an immediate ambush, we dive toward the outer wall.

"Melindra! Reach out on the next turn!"

Melindra holds her breath, eyes watering against the dust, and gives a sharp nod. As the cyclone carries her around in a tight arc, she thrusts both arms outward. Flower and I each seize a wrist and heave with all our weight, tearing her free of the vortex into open air. We fly twenty yards clear before easing down to let her recover.

"Took you long enough..." Melindra coughs violently, spitting grit from her mouth.

"Had to verify the squad was still inside so we didn't walk into a counter-strike," I say.

"Right, right," she wheezes, rubbing her eyes. "Glad to see you got the message, at least."

"Understood eventually," I say. "A little more detail wouldn't have hurt."

"I couldn't risk it," Melindra says. "Aura-readers in that squad would have caught a lie in half a heartbeat. I needed genuine panic on your faces for the bluff to hold."

"Could you not cast the storm from our side?" I ask. "Why walk straight into their line?"

"A funnel that size needs an anchor," Melindra says. "If I'm not dead center or right above the eye, the current tears itself apart."

"And now?" I ask. "Can you hold it from here?"

"Should be able to," Melindra mutters. "The initial burst is the hard part. Maintaining it is just—ugh..."

Her hand snaps to her temple, her face twisting in pain.

"Are you alright?" Flower asks.

"Fine, just..." Melindra rubs her forehead with trembling fingers. "Pushed too much mana into the cast. Give me a second and the ache will—oh no."

Below us, three miniature dust devils sprout from the dirt, spinning erratically within spitting distance of Daren and Kate.

"No, no, no!" Melindra's voice spikes. "The stray currents are slipping! I have to drop them, now!"

"Just don't drop the main—"

Before the sentence leaves my mouth, the central tornado collapses into a dead gust. The mini-twisters vanish instantly with it.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Fascinated, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Fascinated)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Fascinated
TEXT:

 The moment the gale drops, Talmak and his squad plummet toward the dirt, coughing and gasping. Twenty feet above the ground, Talmak slams his palms together, manifesting a thick stone slab beneath their boots. They crash onto the rock platform, stumbling as they catch their balance.

"Down to the ground," I tell Flower. "We fight together."

We bank downward, but Melindra remains suspended thirty feet up, motionless, clutching both sides of her skull. Talmak looks up, registers her state instantly, and tears a heavy boulder out of thin air. The rock rockets skyward. Melindra raises a hasty wind barrier, but the spell wavers; the stone punches through the draft and cracks across her skull.

She goes limp, drifting downward.

"Melindra!" Flower dives with blazing fire jets to catch her before she hits the ground.

Talmak hurls a second barrage of stone at Flower, but Illuna's golden cage flares into being around them mid-fall. The boulders shatter against the glowing bars in a spray of gravel.

"The fox's cage..." Talmak mutters, watching Illuna begin channeling white-blue healing light over Melindra's forehead inside the floating lattice.

"You know the golden fox?" I call out.

"I've had more than a few run-ins with her since I came to Varathia, yes," the sage says. "But, more importantly, the situation has now changed. Meet me back down, boy, and we'll have another talk."

He glides his platform to the ground, his squad forming up behind him. I drop down beside Daren and Kate. The moment my boots touch the dirt, the air currents go slack around my limbs, settling into standby until I raise my arms again.

"I've changed my mind!" Talmak announces, raising a palm as weapons clear scabbards. "There is no more need for us to fight."

"And to what do we owe this sudden change of heart?" Daren demands, his blade angled low.

"You owe it to the stillwater girl, up there in the sky," Talmak says, gesturing upward. "You see, I've always been fascinated by the stillwater legends, but I've never really had the chance to see one up close. I've always wanted to study them, and to find out what the source of their power was, but the idea that I would ever have the chance to capture one alive seemed so unlikely, that I never even dared to dream that it might happen. And yet here I am, only a few dozen feet away from an unconscious stillwater, with the only thing standing in my way being that blasted impenetrable cage."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Insane, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Insane)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Insane
TEXT:

 "What exactly are you trying to say, here?" Daren asks, steel in his voice.

"I'm saying that I'm willing to offer you safe passage out of our camp, if you hand over the stillwater," Talmak says. "All you need to do is convince your banshee friend to dismiss the cage and stop healing her, and then we'll forget this encounter ever happened. Nolderan, do we still have those collars we found in the ruins that could restrict someone's ability to cast spells?"

"Yes, we still have them," Nolderan says from the rank behind him.

"Perfect," Talmak says. "Then we can chain her up and commence the experiments immediately. The tricky part will be to keep her alive while we dissect her, but I know more than a few ways to get around--"

A hiss of freezing air cuts him off. Half a dozen razor-sharp icicles spear straight for Talmak's throat.

A slab of bedrock tears upward from the dirt, catching the volley. Shattered frost sprays across the ground, and Talmak glares through the dust at Kate, stunned.

"Have you gone completely insane?" Talmak shouts. "You would forfeit your only chance to survive, just for the sake of a--"

"Stop talking, you filth," Kate says, frost crackling around her clenched fists, pure revulsion in her stare.

"Maybe I haven't made myself sufficiently clear..." Talmak begins, his voice hardening.

"Yeah, no, I think we've heard enough," Hadrik says, and explodes forward in a blur of motion.

Talmak barely raises another stone barrier before Hadrik's fist punches clean through the center, blasting rock fragments in all directions.

"Enough!" Talmak roars. Jagged stone spikes thrust from the earth beneath Hadrik's boots, forcing the dwarf to leap backward. "If you're all so eager to march towards your deaths, then I will be more than happy to grant your wishes. I'm going to kill you all myself, and I'm saving the stillwater for last."

"No, you won't," Hadrik grins, closing the distance again with a furious flurry of bare-knuckle strikes, forcing Talmak into a desperate rhythm of conjuring wall after wall to absorb the impacts.

Behind the sage, the five bodyguards draw their stances and advance on the rest of us.

choice("Focus my attacks on the henchmen", B2-Ch10b-Henchmen, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Henchmen, v_b2_ch10_target = 0)
choice("Go attack the sage, together with Hadrik", B2-Ch10b-Dwarf, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Dwarf, v_b2_ch10_target = 1)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Dwarf
TEXT:

set(v_is_dead,1) if (v_toughness == 4 && v_strength < 4 || v_toughness < 4)
set(v_is_dead,0) if (v_strength == 4 && v_toughness == 4)
#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0 && v_toughness < 4) {
 

Crossbow bolts won't bite through the layered physical wards wrapping Talmak's frame. Close quarters with steel is the only angle.

As the bodyguards peel off to intercept Daren, Kate, and Leila, I throw my arms up. The air currents grip me, launching me straight at the sage with my dagger drawn.

Talmak spots the line and heaves a slab of solid rock from the dirt to bar my path. I drive the blade forward, but the edge wedges half an inch into reinforced stone and jams. The mineral density has been magically hardened, dense as Kate's reinforced frost.

Without maxed strength and toughness to punch through stone, my arm would shatter on impact. I yank the dagger free and bank wide around the barrier, hunting another seam.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0 && v_toughness == 4 && v_strength < 4) {
 

Crossbow bolts won't bite through the layered physical wards wrapping Talmak's frame. Close quarters with steel is the only angle.

As the bodyguards peel off to intercept Daren, Kate, and Leila, I throw my arms up. The air currents grip me, launching me straight at the sage with my dagger drawn.

Talmak spots the line and heaves a slab of solid rock from the dirt to bar my path. I drive the blade forward, but the edge wedges half an inch into reinforced stone and jams. The mineral density has been magically hardened, dense as Kate's reinforced frost.

Without maxed strength and toughness to punch through stone, my arm would shatter on impact. I yank the dagger free and bank wide around the barrier, hunting another seam.

}
#if(v_b2_ch5_scimitar == 0 && v_strength == 4 && v_toughness == 4) {
 

Crossbow bolts won't bite through the layered physical wards wrapping Talmak's frame. Close quarters with steel is the only angle.

As the bodyguards peel off to intercept Daren, Kate, and Leila, I throw my arms up. The air currents grip me, launching me straight at the sage with my dagger drawn.

Talmak spots the line and heaves a slab of solid rock from the dirt to bar my path. I drive the blade forward, but the edge wedges half an inch into reinforced stone and jams. The mineral density has been magically hardened, dense as Kate's reinforced frost.

I wrench the blade free, drop it into the sheath, and drive my boosted fist straight through the center of the slab, shattering it to dust just like Hadrik.

}
#if(v_toughness < 4 && v_b2_ch5_scimitar != 0) {
 

Crossbow bolts won't bite through the layered physical wards wrapping Talmak's frame. Close quarters with steel is the only angle.

As the bodyguards peel off to intercept Daren, Kate, and Leila, I throw my arms up. The air currents grip me, launching me straight at the sage with my scimitar drawn.

Talmak spots the line and heaves a slab of solid rock from the dirt to bar my path. I slash the curved blade into the rock, but the edge catches and binds in the stone. The mineral density has been magically hardened, dense as Kate's reinforced frost.

Without maxed strength and toughness to punch through stone, my arm would shatter on impact. I yank the scimitar free and bank wide around the barrier, hunting another seam.

}
#if(v_toughness == 4 && v_strength < 4 && v_b2_ch5_scimitar != 0) {
 

Crossbow bolts won't bite through the layered physical wards wrapping Talmak's frame. Close quarters with steel is the only angle.

