ID: B2-Ch10b-Shocked
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch10_target == 0) {
 The blond elf snaps a kinetic barrier under his own boots and extends a second platform under Nolderan. Below us, Kate conjures a sequence of frost shelves to break our fall, but before we hit the ice, air currents grip us around the waist, lifting Kate, Leila, Daren, Hadrik, and me back toward the surface.

Thirty feet above the rift, Melindra hovers with outstretched palms, directing the draft with slow, deliberate gestures, her face locked in intense concentration.

"Melindra!" Flower calls out, banking away from the dark-haired banshee's pursuit. "You shouldn't be casting heavy spells right after a concussion!"

"I'm fine," Melindra grunts, hauling us over the rim. "Worry about the sage."

High above the compound, the churned bedrock coalesces into a towering fifty-foot golem of solid stone. Talmak stands rooted within the giant's hollow cranium, riding his stone platform like a command deck.

"Absorb this with your sword, Daren!" Talmak's voice booms from fifty feet up. "See if you can scratch the sole of its boot before it crushes you into paste!"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_target == 1) {
 The blond elf snaps kinetic barriers under his boots, Nolderan, and the two fire dwarves. Below us, Kate conjures a sequence of frost shelves to break our fall, but before we hit the ice, air currents grip us around the waist, lifting Kate, Leila, Daren, Hadrik, and me back toward the surface.

Thirty feet above the rift, Melindra hovers with outstretched palms, directing the draft with slow, deliberate gestures, her face locked in intense concentration.

"Melindra!" Flower calls out, banking away from the dark-haired banshee's pursuit. "You shouldn't be casting heavy spells right after a concussion!"

"I'm fine," Melindra grunts, hauling us over the rim. "Worry about the sage."

High above the compound, the churned bedrock coalesces into a towering fifty-foot golem of solid stone. Talmak stands rooted within the giant's hollow cranium, riding his stone platform like a command deck.

"Absorb this with your sword, Daren!" Talmak's voice booms from fifty feet up. "See if you can scratch the sole of its boot before it crushes you into paste!"

}
 Talmak drives his palms together. The jagged chasm walls grind shut with a shuddering groan, sealing the fissure back into solid earth beneath the golem's feet.

Melindra glides our group fifty paces back from the colossus and eases us onto the dirt.

"Healer, barrier up," Melindra barks from above. "Layer everything you've got. What I'm about to cast doesn't come with fine steering."

"Right," Daren raises his shield, projecting a thick, glowing aegis over our perimeter.

Melindra climbs twenty paces higher into clear sky. "Flower, clear the airspace! Take your fight across the forest—I'm not holding back!"

"Got it!" Flower shouts. "Over here, banshee!"

Flower fires her thrusters, rocketing north over the tree line with the dark-haired banshee in furious pursuit.

Once the sky clears, Melindra crosses both wrists.

A high, metallic hum vibrates through the air. Hundreds of keen-edged wind blades form in a dense orbit around her silhouette, spinning with blurring speed. The blades shimmer with refracted sunlight, edges honed to the same lethal density as Kate's sharpened frost.

The stone colossus takes a heavy step forward, the earth shaking under its stride as Talmak's squad scrambles clear of its path.

A stray wind blade breaks formation, shearing downward at blinding speed. It slices through the dirt three feet in front of Daren's ward, carving a four-foot trench into bedrock like warm wax.

"Damn..." Daren mutters, staring at the clean slit in the stone. "Let's hope the ward holds against a direct hit..."

choice(""Would your barrier work more effectively if we huddled closer together?"", B2-Ch10b-Huddled, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Huddled)
choice(""Maybe you could try to use your sword to absorb the wind blades."", B2-Ch10b-Absorb, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Absorb)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Huddled
TEXT:

 "Would your barrier work more effectively if we huddled closer together?"

"Yes—less surface area means denser coverage," Daren says. "Close in!"

We pack tight shoulder to shoulder; Daren pulls the perimeter in, condensing the shield into a tight, brilliant dome of white light.

Fifty feet up, Melindra's voice drifts down, eerily calm. "I heard what you said earlier, Talmak. All that talk about chaining me in collars and keeping me alive for dissection. Bold words for someone talking to a stillwater."

"You're too late, girl!" Talmak shouts from the golem's skull. "Tornadoes won't scratch this mass. Whatever little draft tricks you're spinning won't save you."

"Is that what you think?..." Melindra murmurs.

"I have no use for dead specimens," Talmak sneers. "Die where you float!"

The golem's right arm heaves upward, a stone fist the size of a wagon driving toward Melindra to crush her out of the sky.

The moment the stone nears her orbit, the cloud of wind blades detonates into motion. Hundreds of invisible edges shred into the fist, reducing tons of bedrock to a fine spray of sand in two seconds flat.

Talmak recoils in horror, staring at the stump of the golem's arm. "No..."

"Allow me to demonstrate, Sage Talmak," Melindra's voice rings like crystal, "why air reigns supreme."

She throws both arms wide.

Thousands of lethal wind blades erupt from the atmosphere, blotting out the sky in a blinding kaleidoscope of shimmering, sunlit edges. The swarm descends on the stone giant like a thresher.

The fifty-foot golem dissolves in a hurricane of dust. Talmak frantically summons bedrock to patch the torso, but each new boulder is sliced into ribbons the instant it leaves the ground.

A hail of stray wind blades batters Daren's compressed barrier, ringing like hammer blows against iron, but the condensed shield holds firm.

Stripped of his golem, Talmak drops onto his floating platform, frantically heaving up a fortified stone bunker around himself as the air storm carves into the rock.

"Delvram!" Talmak screams over the roar of shredding stone. "Get us out! Now!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Misinterpreting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Misinterpreting)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Absorb
TEXT:

 "Maybe you could try to use your sword to absorb the wind blades."

"Thought of that," Daren grunts, "but if my timing slips by half an inch, the edge takes my forearm off."

"What if you hold the blade through the ward while keeping your arm inside?" I suggest. "The barrier protects your grip."

"Worth a shot," Daren says, thrusting the broadsword's tip two feet through the glowing white field.

Fifty feet up, Melindra's voice drifts down, eerily calm. "I heard what you said earlier, Talmak. All that talk about chaining me in collars and keeping me alive for dissection. Bold words for someone talking to a stillwater."

"You're too late, girl!" Talmak shouts from the golem's skull. "Tornadoes won't scratch this mass. Whatever little draft tricks you're spinning won't save you."

"Is that what you think?..." Melindra murmurs.

"I have no use for dead specimens," Talmak sneers. "Die where you float!"

The golem's right arm heaves upward, a stone fist the size of a wagon driving toward Melindra to crush her out of the sky.

The moment the stone nears her orbit, the cloud of wind blades detonates into motion. Hundreds of invisible edges shred into the fist, reducing tons of bedrock to a fine spray of sand in two seconds flat.

Talmak recoils in horror, staring at the stump of the golem's arm. "No..."

"Allow me to demonstrate, Sage Talmak," Melindra's voice rings like crystal, "why air reigns supreme."

She throws both arms wide.

Thousands of lethal wind blades erupt from the atmosphere, blotting out the sky in a blinding kaleidoscope of shimmering, sunlit edges. The swarm descends on the stone giant like a thresher.

The fifty-foot golem dissolves in a hurricane of dust. Talmak frantically summons bedrock to patch the torso, but each new boulder is sliced into ribbons the instant it leaves the ground.

Stray wind blades hammer Daren's barrier. Daren sweeps his protruding blade through the incoming paths, absorbing several air constructs on contact and easing the strain on the white aegis.

Stripped of his golem, Talmak drops onto his floating platform, frantically heaving up a fortified stone bunker around himself as the air storm carves into the rock.

"Delvram!" Talmak screams over the roar of shredding stone. "Get us out! Now!"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Misinterpreting, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Misinterpreting)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Misinterpreting
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch10_target == 0) {
 "As you wish, Sage Talmak..." the elf chants, weaving a complex teleportation matrix across his squad.

Melindra redirects a volley of wind blades at his position, but Delvram's personal kinetic ward deflects the incoming storm.

"No!" the enemy banshee shrieks, her body vibrating with blinding teleportation light. "Leave me! I'm not done with—"

A crack of displaced air swallows the sentence. In a flash of white light, Talmak, Nolderan, Delvram, and the banshee vanish from the compound.

Melindra dispels the blade swarm with a flick of her wrist and glides down to the dirt as Flower banks back into the clearing.

"Wow..." Daren lowers his broadsword, staring at the pulverized rubble. "That was... truly something."

"Nice try, healer," Melindra says, landing with her arms crossed, eyes narrowed with suspicion. "We both know what's running through that head of yours. Now that the secret is out and you know I can't steer my magic, you're already drafting containment protocols so you don't get shredded next time."

"I mean, sure, we need caution..." Daren fumbles. "But I don't see why—"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_target == 1) {
 "As you wish, Sage Talmak..." the elf chants, weaving a complex teleportation matrix across his squad.

Melindra redirects a volley of wind blades at his position, but Delvram's personal kinetic ward deflects the incoming storm.

"No!" the enemy banshee shrieks, her body vibrating with blinding teleportation light. "Leave me! I'm not done with—"

A crack of displaced air swallows the sentence. In a flash of white light, Talmak, Nolderan, Delvram, the banshee, and the two fire dwarves vanish from the compound.

Melindra dispels the blade swarm with a flick of her wrist and glides down to the dirt as Flower banks back into the clearing.

"Wow..." Daren lowers his broadsword, staring at the pulverized rubble. "That was... truly something."

"Nice try, healer," Melindra says, landing with her arms crossed, eyes narrowed with suspicion. "We both know what's running through that head of yours. Now that the secret is out and you know I can't steer my magic, you're already drafting containment protocols so you don't get shredded next time."

"I mean, sure, we need caution..." Daren fumbles. "But I don't see why—"

}
 "Let me ask plainly," Melindra snaps. "Are you planning to weave a limiter spell over my aura?"

"What?..." Daren stares at her, genuinely appalled. "No! Of course not! What are you talking about? Do you know the lethal backlash of attempting a white-magic limiter on an unaligned stillwater aura? It would rip your channels apart. I would never even consider such a thing!"

"You wouldn't?..." Melindra blinks, caught off balance.

"Listen," Daren's voice softens. "I understand why you kept quiet. We didn't give you much reason to trust us, and trust goes both ways. But you made the right call holding back. You shouldn't risk engaging in routine skirmishes unless there's no other path. The risk to everyone—including you—is too high."

"Agreed," Kate adds, stepping up beside Daren. "Even without hostile intent, a single stray wind blade grazing any of us could trigger your blood oath and kill you on the spot. Unless we're cornered by another sage, we handle the front line."

Kate pauses, meeting Melindra's eyes with rare hesitation.

"And... I apologize for misjudging your actions over the past few days," Kate murmurs. "I see now that you were protecting yourself, not setting us up."

Melindra stumbles back half a pace, staring between them in absolute horror. "Barry, what did you do to them? They're not acting right! Did your stat device cast some kind of mind-control charm?"

"They're just being reasonable, Melindra," I say. "Take the win. And can we clear out of this fortress before a hundred pinecone harvesters rally on our heads?"

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Empty, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Empty, v_b2_ch11_added_hearing = 0)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Empty
TEXT:

 "Good thinking," Daren says, scanning the perimeter. "What about the sage and master Nolderan? Could they be circling back?"

"They're gone," Arraka's voice drifts from the amulet. "Along with the rest of their squad. Their traces snapped straight to their permanent base—the real one, not an empty clearing with a table in the dirt."

"Let's move," Daren says. "We'll debrief on the march."

We file out through the ruined stone gate. The pinecone harvesters have scattered into the deep woods, long cleared out after the colossus rose.

"So just to be completely clear..." Melindra says as we fall into stride beneath the canopy. "No one is casting containment spells over my aura."

"None," Daren says.

"And I stay clear of standard skirmishes, exactly like before?"

"Exactly," Daren confirms.

"I have to admit," Melindra glances sideways at him, "this still sounds slightly suspicious. But I suppose your word holds for now... after all, icey did spearhead the assault on my behalf."

"You... noticed that?" Kate's gaze fixes on the trail, her cheeks coloring slightly.

"Couldn't turn my neck in the dirt, but the spell signature was unmistakable," Melindra says, a faint smirk returning. "Thought I had brain damage for a second. Of everyone in this group, Kate throwing the first icicle at a sage was the last thing I had on my card."

"You... just used my name," Kate says, looking over in surprise.

"I suppose I did, icey," Melindra says softly, looking ahead. "I suppose I did..."

"Which direction?" Daren asks.

"Due north for three hours to a kettle lake," Melindra says. "We rest there, then I'll mark the route toward Ollendor."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Secret, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Secret) if (v_b2_ch6_stat == 1 && v_b2_ch9_channel == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Details, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Details) if (v_b2_ch6_stat == 0 && v_b2_ch9_channel == 1 && v_ch11_saved_rose == 0)
choice("Continue", B2-Ch10b-Except, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Except) if (v_b2_ch9_channel == 0 || v_ch11_saved_rose == 1)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Secret
TEXT:

 A faint static chime rings from my belt. Rose's voice cuts through the transceiver, tight and urgent.

"Barry, do you hear me?"

I tap the rune. "I hear you."

"Listen carefully," the revenant says. "Take out your stat device and enter the override sequence for the hearing stat immediately. Do you understand?"

"What?..."

"There's no time to explain! Just do it! Quick!"

The link snaps shut, leaving dead silence on the line.

choice("Activate the secret code", B2-Ch10b-Boost, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Boost, v_b2_ch10_activate = 1)
choice("Don't activate the secret code", B2-Ch10b-Boost, v_current_scene = B2-Ch10b-Boost, v_b2_ch10_activate = 0)


ID: B2-Ch10b-Boost
TEXT:

set(v_ac_b2_ch10_stalker,1) if (v_ch4_spared_dave == 1)
set(v_b2_ch11_added_hearing,1) if (v_b2_ch10_activate == 1 && v_b2_ch11_added_hearing == 0)
set(v_hearing,+3) if (v_b2_ch10_activate == 1 && v_b2_ch11_added_hearing == 0)
set(v_hearing_aux,+3) if (v_b2_ch10_activate == 1 && v_b2_ch11_added_hearing == 0)
achievement("Stalker detected",v_ac_b2_ch10_stalker)
#if(v_b2_ch10_activate == 1) {
 I pull the device from my pocket, keying the sequence Melindra demonstrated two mornings ago. The rune pulses softly; I slip the casing back into my tunic and pick up the pace.

"Was that the revenant?" Daren matches my stride. "What did she want?"

"Told me to punch in the hearing override on my device," I say. "Didn't see any harm in it, so I keyed the rune."

"Let's hope it's not a trap," Daren mutters.

"Just a temporary boost," I say. "Usually fades in—"

The expected drop-off doesn't come. The sensory sharpness holds steady, every distant leaf-fall and footstep ringing crystal-clear in my ears. Melindra's override was supposed to collapse after a few seconds; this channel is locked open.

"What's wrong, Barry?" Daren asks, catching my pause.

"The stat boost..." I whisper. "It's not resetting. It's holding permanently."

"Hardware glitch?" Daren frowns.

"Not with these devices," I say.

Illuna's voice drifts from the rear of the column, sharp and low.

"Arraka, are there any mages left in this sector?"

}
#if(v_b2_ch10_activate == 0) {
 I leave the device in my pocket. Taking unverified commands from a dead voice on an open frequency is a fast way to get walked into an ambush.

"Was that the revenant?" Daren matches my stride. "What did she want?"

"Demanded I enter a stat code without explaining why," I say. "I closed the channel."

"Let me know if she rings back," Daren says.

"Sure."

We hike in silence for another quarter-mile until Illuna's voice drifts from the rear of the column, sharp and low.

"Arraka, are there any mages left in this sector?"

}
#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 0) {
 "Mages?" Arraka muses. "No, not any mages..."

"What do you mean by that?" Illuna presses. "Monsters? Beasts?"

"Maybe," Arraka purrs.

"You are testing my patience," Illuna snaps.

The forest goes completely still. The canopy breeze dies mid-rustle. All birdsong, insect hum, and background rustle vanish in an instant. A few yards ahead, two midges hang motionless in mid-air, frozen between wingbeats.

Hadrik breaks the silence with a dry chuckle.

"So, I've been wondering about something for a while... maybe you lot can clear it up for me," Hadrik says. "Does the time look frozen to anyone else? Or is it just me?..."

}
#if(v_ch4_spared_dave == 1) {
 "Mages?" Arraka laughs. "Oh, there's one! Same little shadow that's been tailing us under an invisibility weave since dawn."

"What?!" Illuna's tone drops to ice. "Someone has been following us all morning? Why didn't you report it?"

"Hardly worth the effort," Arraka sneers. "Even the half-lessathi could break him with one hand tied behind his back. I wanted to see what the little rat was after, so I let him trail along."

"You are testing my patience," Illuna snaps.

The forest goes completely still. The canopy breeze dies mid-rustle. All birdsong, insect hum, and background rustle vanish in an instant. A few yards ahead, two midges hang motionless in mid-air, frozen between wingbeats.

Hadrik breaks the silence with a dry chuckle.

"So, I've been wondering about something for a while... maybe you lot can clear it up for me," Hadrik says. "Does the time look frozen to anyone else? Or is it just me?..."

}
